this is the World Wide Web a giant  decentralized network connecting  billions of people across the world the  source of much of the world's freest and  most innovative content shaped and  improved by the world's freest and most  innovative minds yet designed and  controlled by no single person company  or government the internet functions by  sending small clusters of information  called data packets from one computer to  another this data packet transmission is  how you are able to read online  newspapers download songs or watch  online videos internet service providers  or ISPs own the networks of wire and  cable through which these packets travel  and for the use of these travel ways the  companies that own them charge users a  fee usually a flat monthly fee in some  countries like China and Pakistan the  government puts up blocks along these  travel ways because some of the packets  have information that officials don't  want people to see but in the US this  interconnected system has stayed very  open and free because the government has  stayed hands-off and the companies have  incentive to cooperate with each other  so that each of their networks keeps the  data flowing as quickly and smoothly as  possible  but now that freedom we enjoy in the  u.s. is threatened thanks to the efforts  of the Federal Communications Commission  to impose so-called net neutrality rules  net neutrality advocates claim that  without their rules  ISPs will impede access to content  provided by companies they don't like or  view as competition net neutrality would  correct this situation by forcing ISPs  to be neutral by offering equal  accessibility to all online content but  the problem is this nightmare scenario  net neutrality advocate deposit has  never actually happened if AT&amp;T DSL  blocked your access to Google because  they wanted you to use Yahoo what would  you do probably cancel your plan and go  to a provider that gives you easy access  to your favorite sites neutrality is  what most customers want  which is exactly why ISPs have stayed  neutral without a single government  regulation telling them to do so  net neutrality types also worried that  companies will charge some users for the  amount of data they use instead of a  flat monthly fee some companies might do  this but it could actually result in  cheaper prices for those users who don't  download several feature-length movies a  day or run a video hosting website after  all someone has to pay for the increased  use of bandwidth just like someone has  to pay for the increased electricity  used by the mansion down the street or  by the local mall yet we pay for our  electricity use as individuals or  households not by subsidizing the use of  everyone in the country and for those  customers who do on a flat monthly fee  competing companies will likely  recognize that desire and offer services  to meet it the same way many of these  same companies offer varied cellphone  plans customized to the user not  designed as a one-size-fits-all standard  by the government but the larger point  is nobody predicted how the internet  would evolve who knows what the internet  could look like in 5-10 years if it's  left free to continue evolving allowing  service providers to continue innovating  freely can only improve the level of  service while clumsy FCC regulation yes  the same FCC that levies massive fines  for nipple slips and fleeting expletives  on live TV will only stunt the  internet's development not to mention it  will give the US government and its many  many lobbyists unprecedented power over  the freest medium of expression still  existing in the world  hmm what could go wrong  you
 

